GNSS: the vendor library OH's GNSS HDI loads (vendorGnssAdapter.so, symbol GnssVendorInterface), which the port did
not have, so OH's location service had no GNSS behind it.

The SDM845 modem holds the GNSS engine; its QMI LOC service (0x10) is reached over QRTR (the modem's helpers rmtfs,
pd-mapper and tqftpserv run on the board). The library is a small QMI LOC client: it finds LOC through QRTR's name
service, registers for position reports, NMEA and engine state, sets standalone operation and runs periodic fix
sessions (QMI LOC Start/Stop). Fixes come from the position reports, satellites from the NMEA GSV sentences.
Built as C++ (the OH header uses enum class) without the C++ runtime; it needs the build whitelist entry of
patches/oh/build/0003. No AGNSS, geofence or measurements (getGnssModuleIface returns none).

Checked on the phone (OH 7.0): with Location on, the GNSS host loads the library and finds QMI LOC at node 0 port 80;
a test session reports engine on and NMEA once a second; Wi-Fi stays up during sessions. A fix needs sky view (the
test phone is indoors: no satellites in view).

